3006:
For 3 \times 3 matrices adj(cM) = c^{2} adj(M), and (adj A)^{-1} = \frac{A}{\det A}
Here \det A = -3+11-12 = -4, so 2(adj A)^{-1} = -\frac{A}{2}
Taking adjoints twice gives adj(adj(-\frac{A}{2})) = -\frac{A}{4}, whose entries sum to -\frac{12}{4} = -3
This sum matches option D → (D)

3009:
The powers (1+x)^{3} through (1+x)^{99} form a geometric run summing to \frac{(1+x)^{100}-(1+x)^{3}}{x}
Reading the x^{3} coefficient there means reading x^{4} upstairs, giving \binom{100}{4} = \frac{97}{4}\binom{100}{3}
Adding the (1+kx)^{100} share k^{3}\binom{100}{3} and equating to (43n+\frac{101}{4})\binom{100}{3} yields k^{3} = 43n+1
The smallest k in N with n \geq 1 is k = 6 with n = 5, so p+n = 11
This sum matches option B → (B)

3014:
Write P = (2t,3t,-1+3t) on the first line and Q = (-1-s,2+s,4s) on the second
The join PQ must be parallel to (1,-1,2), giving t = \frac{1}{5} and s = -\frac{8}{15}
Then PQ = -\frac{13}{15}(1,-1,2), so l^{2} = \frac{169 \times 6}{225} = \frac{1014}{225}
Thus 225l^{2} = 1014, which matches option B → (B)

3017:
Degrees give n = (n-1)+(n-2), so n = 3, and with f(0) = 0 write f = ax^{3}+bx^{2}+cx
Expanding f'f'' and matching coefficients forces a = \frac{1}{18} with b = c = 0, so f = \frac{x^{3}}{18}
Then f'(2) = f''(2) = \frac{2}{3} and \int_{0}^{2}f = \frac{2}{9}, summing to \frac{14}{9}
Multiplying by 36 gives 56, which matches option C → (C)

3023:
Since \frac{\sin A}{\cos 3A} = \frac{1}{2}(\tan 3A-\tan A) from the sine subtraction formula, each term telescopes
Summing gives A = \frac{1}{2}(\tan 81\theta-\tan 3\theta) = \frac{B}{2}
Hence \frac{B}{A} = 2 → (2)

3024:
Put \theta_{k+1} = 2\theta_{k} with \theta_{1} = \frac{\pi}{2^{n}+1}; then |a_{k}|^{2} = \sec^{2}\theta_{k} and |b_{k}|^{2} = \csc^{2}\theta_{k}
From \sin 2\theta = 2\sin\theta\cos\theta comes \sec^{2}\theta_{k}+\csc^{2}\theta_{k} = 4\csc^{2}\theta_{k+1}
Summing over k telescopes, and \theta_{n+1} = \pi-\theta_{1} shares its cosecant, leaving \sum\sec^{2} = 3\sum\csc^{2}
Hence the ratio equals 3 → (3)

3026:
Pitch is linear distance per rotation, Pitch = \frac{2.5 \text{ mm}}{5} = 0.5 \text{ mm}
Least count is pitch per circular division, LC = \frac{0.5 \text{ mm}}{100} = 0.005 \text{ mm}
Converting to powers of ten gives 5 \times 10^{-3} \text{ mm} → (D)

3027:
Bulk modulus relates pressure change to volume strain, B = \frac{\Delta P}{\Delta V/V}
Volume strain is therefore \frac{\Delta V}{V} = \frac{6.3 \times 10^{7}}{2.1 \times 10^{9}} = 0.03
Percentage decrease is 0.03 \times 100 = 3 \text{ percent} → (B)

3028:
Acceleration on smooth 45^{\circ} incline is g \sin 45^{\circ} and on rough incline is g(\sin 45^{\circ} - \mu_{k} \cos 45^{\circ})
Same slide length gives time ratio \frac{t_{rough}}{t_{smooth}} = \sqrt{\frac{a_{smooth}}{a_{rough}}} = \frac{3}{2}
Hence \frac{1}{1 - \mu_{k}} = \frac{9}{4}, giving \mu_{k} = \frac{5}{9} → (C)

3029:
Total initial binding energy is 2 \times 3 \times 5.6 + 4 \times 7.4 = 63.2 \text{ MeV}
Final binding energy is 10 \times 6.1 = 61 \text{ MeV}
Energy deficit appears as mass gain, \Delta M c^{2} = 63.2 - 61 = 2.2 \text{ MeV} → (C)

3030:
Same density gives small sphere radius from volume ratio, r^{3} = \frac{R^{3}}{8} so r = \frac{R}{2}
Small sphere about diameter has I_{1} = \frac{2}{5} \times \frac{M}{8} \times \left(\frac{R}{2}\right)^{2} = \frac{MR^{2}}{80}
Disc of mass \frac{7M}{8} and radius 2R about a diameter has I_{2} = \frac{1}{4} \times \frac{7M}{8} \times (2R)^{2} = \frac{7MR^{2}}{8}, so \frac{I_{2}}{I_{1}} = 70 → (B)

3031:
Range at speed u is R = \frac{u^{2} \sin 2\theta}{g}
Ratio for 15^{\circ} and 30^{\circ} is \frac{R_{1}}{R_{2}} = \frac{\sin 30^{\circ}}{\sin 60^{\circ}} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}
Writing this ratio as 1 : x gives x = \sqrt{3} → (B)

3032:
Einstein equation gives maximum kinetic energy K_{max} = hf - \phi with threshold f_{0} = \frac{\phi}{h}
Stopping potential graph has same slope \frac{h}{e} and f-intercept equal to threshold frequency f_{0}
Metal X_{1} has smallest f_{0} so smallest work function and largest K_{max} for same incident f → (A)

3033:
Minima of single slit satisfy a \sin \theta = m\lambda with position y_{m} = \frac{mD\lambda}{a}
First minimum is at y_{1} = \frac{D\lambda}{a} and third minimum at y_{3} = \frac{3D\lambda}{a}
Separation is y_{3} - y_{1} = \frac{2D\lambda}{a} → (C)

3035:
Mole conservation gives n = n_{1} + n_{2} for ideal gases
Using n = \frac{PV}{RT} gives \frac{PV}{T} = \frac{P_{1}V_{1}}{T_{1}} + \frac{P_{2}V_{2}}{T_{2}}
Solving for mixture temperature gives T = \frac{T_{1}T_{2}PV}{T_{2}P_{1}V_{1} + T_{1}P_{2}V_{2}} → (B)

3036:
Process obeys P = \frac{P_{0}}{1 + (V_{0}/V)^{2}} with n = 2 so T = \frac{PV}{2R}
At V_{0}, P_{A} = \frac{P_{0}}{2} giving T_{A} = \frac{P_{0}V_{0}}{4R}
At 3V_{0}, P_{B} = \frac{9P_{0}}{10} giving T_{B} = \frac{27P_{0}V_{0}}{20R}, so T_{B} - T_{A} = \frac{11P_{0}V_{0}}{10R} → (B)

3037:
Full-scale current is fixed by coil, I_{g} = \frac{20}{x}
Higher range needs series multiplier, V = I_{g}(x + R_{s})
Setting 30 = \frac{20}{x}(x + R_{s}) gives R_{s} = \frac{x}{2} in series → (A)

3040:
Battery holds V constant while C = \frac{\epsilon_{0}A}{x}
Stored energy is U = \frac{1}{2}CV^{2} = \frac{\epsilon_{0}AV^{2}}{2x}
Differentiating at speed v = \frac{dx}{dt} gives \frac{dU}{dt} \propto x^{-2}, so \alpha = -2 → (A)

3044:
Diffraction-limited resolution is \theta = \frac{1.22\lambda}{D}
Rearranging gives aperture D = \frac{1.22\lambda}{\theta}
Substituting \lambda = 500 nm and \theta = 5 \times 10^{-7} gives D = 1.22 m = 122 cm → (B)

3046:
Net force is vector sum, \vec{F} = (2 + 3)\hat{i} + (3 - 1)\hat{j} + (4 - 2)\hat{k} = 5\hat{i} + 2\hat{j} + 2\hat{k} in SI units
Displacement is 25 m along unit vector \hat{u} = \frac{3\hat{i} - 4\hat{j}}{5}, so \vec{d} = 15\hat{i} - 20\hat{j}
Work is dot product, W = \vec{F} \cdot \vec{d} = 75 - 40 = 35 J → (35)

3049:
Acceleration is \vec{a} = \frac{\vec{F}}{m} = t\hat{i} + 3t^{2}\hat{j} in SI units
Starting from rest gives \vec{v} = \frac{t^{2}}{2}\hat{i} + t^{3}\hat{j}, so at t = 2, \vec{F} = 4\hat{i} + 24\hat{j} and \vec{v} = 2\hat{i} + 8\hat{j}
Instantaneous power P = \vec{F} \cdot \vec{v} = 8 + 192 = 200 W → (200)
